# What the lifecycle test leaves out when it compares the files of a system before the setup and
# after the removal (scripts/lifecycle-snapshot.sh, scripts/lifecycle-test.sh). One path to a
# line, then why. Everything below a path is left out; the path itself is compared. A path that
# ends in * is a name that starts that way. Nothing of SpliceFS may be listed here: a file of
# ours that stays behind is what the test is for. A log file of ours below /var/log is compared
# although /var/log is left out (scripts/lifecycle-snapshot.sh).
/var/log          logs: the system and the package managers write them while they run
/var/cache        caches of the package managers
/var/lib/apt      the package manager's own state: the index apt-get update downloads
/var/lib/dpkg     the package manager's own state: the dpkg database and its backups
/var/lib/dnf      the package manager's own state: dnf's transaction history
/var/lib/rpm      the package manager's own state: the rpm database
/lib/apk          the package manager's own state: the apk database and its scripts
/var/lib/NetworkManager  the state NetworkManager writes by itself while the system runs (Rocky Linux)
/var/lib/chrony  the clock drift chronyd writes by itself while the system runs (openSUSE)
/var/lib/snapd/maintenance.json*  the note snapd writes by itself while it does its own maintenance (Ubuntu)
/var/lib/update-motd  the temporary files of the message-of-the-day updater, which runs on its own timer (Amazon Linux)
/var/tmp/systemd-private-*  the private temporary folders systemd makes for its services, named at random
/etc/ld.so.cache*  the cache ldconfig writes of the libraries it finds; it is rewritten when any package installs a library
/etc/passwd-*     the backup that useradd and userdel make of the account file: it holds the state before the last change, so it differs after any user was added and deleted
/etc/shadow-*     the same backup of the password file
/etc/group-*      the same backup of the group file
/etc/gshadow-*    the same backup of the group password file
